  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to represent the step function as a Fourier series with fixed integration constraint and optimize the coefficients to minimize the maximum cross-correlation integral. Unlike the parent approach that used coordinate descent and simplex method, this method:\n1. Uses low-frequency Fourier series to reduce oscillation and maintain feasibility\n2. Imposes periodic behavior inherent to the domain [0,2]\n3. Automatically satisfies integral constraint\n4. More direct control over correlation structure through frequency selection\n\nThe main bottleneck is handling the [0,1] and integral constraints directly. J=2 and J=3 are chosen to balance between complexity and performance, as higher J values require more optimization time without proportional gains. This approach generates smoother functions than comb-like constructions, which helps maintain lower correlation values.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: h(x) = 0.5 + \u2211_{j=1}^{J} (a_j cos(j\u03c0x) + b_j sin(j\u03c0x))\n- Variables: Coefficients a_j, b_j \u2208 [-0.4,0.4] for J=2,3 \n- Objective: C5 = (2/n) * max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full'))\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x, \u222bh dx=1 (automatically satisfied)\n- Relaxation: Clip function values to [0,1] for nonfeasible candidates\n- Initial: h(x) = 0.5 + 0.2 sin(\u03c0x) (J=1) as seed\n\n3. Implementation procedure\ndef build_h(x, a1, b1, a2=None, b2=None):\n    h = 0.5 * np.ones(len(x))\n    if b1 is not None:\n        h += a1*np.cos(np.pi*x) + b1*np.sin(np.pi*x)\n    if a2 is not None:\n        h += a2*np.cos(2*np.pi*x) + b2*np.sin(2*np.pi*x)\n    return h\n\ndef cost_function(params, n):\n    try:\n        if len(params)==2:\n            a1, b1 = params\n            x = np.linspace(0,2,n)\n            h_full = build_h(x, a1, b1)\n            if np.min(h_full) < 0 or np.max(h_full)>1:\n                return 1e9 + 1000*(np.abs(h_full.clip(0,1)-0.5).sum()**2)\n            h_clip = h_full.clip(0,1)\n            corr_val = np.max(np.correlate(h_clip,1-h_clip,mode='full'))\n            return (2/n)*corr_val + 10*(np.abs(h_full.clip(0,1)-0.5).sum()**2/n)\n        elif len(params)==4:\n            a1,b1,a2,b2 = params\n            x = np.linspace(0,2,n)\n            h_full = build_h(x, a1, b1, a2,b2)\n            if np.min(h_full)<0 or np.max(h_full)>1:\n                return 1e9 + 1000*(np.abs(h_full.clip(0,1)-0.5).sum()**2)\n            h_clip = h_full.clip(0,1)\n            corr_val = np.max(np.correlate(h_clip,1-h_clip,mode='full'))\n            return (2/n)*corr_val + 10*(np.abs(h_full.clip(0,1)-0.5).sum()**2/n)\n    except Exception as e:\n        return 1e9\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nJ=2: [a1,b1]\nJ=3: [a1,b1,a2,b?] (add even component)\n\nGrid optimization parameters:\n- n_points: always 700 (current grid)\n- J_min,J_max: 1 to 4\n- Min iteration: 200\n- Max time per J: 10 seconds\n\nFallback: Use parent's J=1 solution if plateaued\n\n5. Budget and robustness\n- Total budget: 1100 seconds\n- Level of parallelism: 1 (sequential)\n- Time checks: Every optimization run after 5 seconds\n- Starting configurations:\n   J=1: [0,0.2]\n   J=2: parent_fourier (from initial h_values)\n   J=3: [0.01,-0.05,0.01,-0.05]\n   J=4: [0.0,-0.05,0.01,-0.02,-0.01,0.02,0.01,-0.03]\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Output format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- Convert Fourier function to grid: Sample build_h(x=..., a1,b1) at 700 points\n- Validation: check sum(h)*dx=1 (with dx=2/n) and 0<=h_i<=1\n- Metrics: c5_bound = C5 value (smaller is better)\n- Special case: If time<5s, fall back to parent's solution",
